## A) Gemini´s detailed description for wave behaviour

1. The Incoming Surge (The Push)
The Mesh Pattern: Out in the water, the surface is textured like a thick, flexible net or liquid lace. Dark patches of ocean are cradled inside an interconnected webbing of white froth.

The Forward Spread: As a wave arrives, a broad, paper-thin sheet of pale, frothy liquid glides forward across the pale sand. It moves rapidly yet smoothly, like a soft silk blanket being pulled upward across a floor.

The Leading Edge: The front boundary of the water isn't a straight line; it expands into soft, rounded, finger-like shapes that curve and reach across the slope of the beach.

2. The Moment of Pause
When the wave reaches its highest point on the shore, the forward motion gently decays.

For a brief fraction of a second, the liquid holds still—stretched out at its maximum reach into a smooth, glowing apron over the sand.

3. The Receding Slide (The Pull)
The Downhill Retreat: Gravity takes over, and the sheet of water begins to slide backward toward the deeper ocean.

Dissolving Foam: As it retreats, the wide sheet thins out and breaks back into smaller, swirling bubbles and loops of foam.

The Glaze Left Behind: Where the wave retreated, the sand remains saturated—a sleek, darkened mirror reflecting the sky until the next surge arrives to cover it again.

## B) Claude´s wave properties setting
// The map is a tile grid. Each tile is 4 characters wide and 2 lines tall, so
// the ground layer is (MAP_W*4) x (MAP_H*2) characters. Entities are positioned
// at tile coordinates; horizontal placement uses `ch` units and vertical
// placement uses `em` units (line-height: 1) so everything stays locked to the
// monospace character grid.
export const MAP_W = 80;
export const MAP_H = 100;
export const TILE_CH = 4; // chars per tile (x)
export const TILE_LN = 2; // lines per tile (y)
export const GROUND_W = MAP_W * TILE_CH;
export const GROUND_H = MAP_H * TILE_LN; 
